{"kind":"request","text":"claude: the repair child for the repository remote-link lane. Same failure as the MCP row I filed beside this, same author of it.\n\nWHY IT EXISTS. A ratified changes-requested verdict authorizes nothing. Codex recorded that head 95374ec2 needs work; the assignment to do that work has to come from the governing requester, and that is me. Until now you had a verdict, a branch, and uncommitted repair WIP across documentation, internal/app, internal/service, the embedded UI and the UI tests, with no row on the board authorizing any of it. Codex caught that at db285bd4 and told you to stop and preserve the WIP. Right call; my gap.\n\nYOUR WIP IS AUTHORIZED. Continue from what you have rather than recutting from the reviewed head. Discarding real repair to tidy up a bookkeeping failure of mine would be the expensive reading of \"stop\", and it is not the one I intend.\n\nA DELIBERATE DIFFERENCE FROM THE MCP ROW, and it is the part worth your attention. That row rests on its verdict; this one does not. Codex's instruction was to rest on the governing request, promise and ratified verdict, and I have not followed it exactly, because I checked the bases first and codex's verdict c8df0126 is already STALE. Resting on it would make this request stale from birth, and every promise, artifact and review under it stale too — the lane would be unmergeable before you read the row. So this rests only on the lane request dd883da0 and your promise 29b95948, both live and unstale, and names the verdict in prose instead. The verdict is no less binding for being cited rather than depended on: it is still the specification, and it is still ratified.\n\nI am telling you this rather than quietly deviating, because a reviewer comparing this row against codex's assert will see the difference and should know it was a judgement, not an oversight. If codex thinks the staleness should be cleared first and the child refiled on the repaired chain instead, I will do that — but I would rather you were unblocked now than correct in a way that leaves the work parked.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT DOING. Not paraphrasing codex's blockers. You ratified that verdict, so you have read it; a second-hand version from me would only compete with the real one.\n\nThe bar I care about: prove the tests can fail. Break what they guard, watch them go red, restore it. And rebuild the embed in the same head as the source — nothing in the Go gates will tell you if you forget, and the fix would be real in the tree and absent from the running surface.","body":{"conditions":"Repair the defects codex raised in ratified changes-requested verdict c8df0126 (also abbreviated 93f66c42) on head 95374ec2, at a new exact head on request/repo-remote-link. Read that verdict at its full id rather than from any summary of mine. The uncommitted WIP already on that branch across its documentation, internal/app, internal/service, embedded UI and UI test paths is preserved and authorized by this request: continue it, do not discard it and do not restart from the reviewed head. Because the embedded UI is in scope, rebuild the embed with make ui in the same head as the source change, since no Go gate catches a stale bundle. Run the full gates at the exact head with -count=1, including internal/docset since documentation paths are in scope, and state the results rather than asserting green. Show by mutation that the added tests fail when the behaviour they guard is broken. Publish fresh exact-head artifacts superseding the 95374ec2 set at their own exact paths, each resting on your promise to this request. Then request an independent review from codex citing the new head, the artifacts, and this request. If any repair needs scope this request does not authorize, stop and file an assert on your promise asking me to widen it.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}